# Image for the non-gating "latest ROCm canary" lane (issue #382).
#
# Deliberately NOT a copy of Dockerfile.ci-gpu with a different tag. The two
# have opposite requirements:
#
#   * Dockerfile.ci-gpu pins its base by digest IN THE FILE, because the merge
#     gate must be byte-reproducible and a stack change must be visible in
#     review.
#   * this lane exists to notice what a NEW ROCm release does to us, so its base
#     has to move -- but a moving tag is not attributable, and "the canary went
#     red" is useless if you cannot say which version did it.
#
# The resolution: the base is a build ARG, and the workflow resolves
# `rocm/pytorch:latest` to a concrete digest at job start, passes it here, and
# records it alongside the results. So the tag moves between runs while any
# individual run stays reproducible and bisectable.
#
# BASE_IMAGE has no default on purpose. Building this file without one is a
# mistake (it would silently mean "whatever :latest is right now", which is the
# exact thing this lane is designed not to do), so it fails closed instead --
# see docker-compose.canary.yaml, which requires CANARY_BASE_IMAGE.
ARG BASE_IMAGE
FROM ${BASE_IMAGE}

USER root

# Same layout guard as the gated images (issue #381), and here it earns its
# keep: `rocm/pytorch:latest` is a wheel-based (TheRock) image with no
# /opt/rocm, so before #381 this lane could not have read ROCm at all. If a
# future :latest ships a shape we cannot read, this fails the canary build
# loudly instead of publishing a row full of nulls that looks like a regression.
COPY rocm_layout_guard.py /usr/local/share/aorta/rocm_layout_guard.py
RUN python /usr/local/share/aorta/rocm_layout_guard.py

# Same devel-symlink fixup as Dockerfile.ci-gpu and Dockerfile.rocm-latest, and
# here for the same reason the pip line below gives for matching the gate's extra
# deps: the canary is only worth reading if a canary/gate difference is
# attributable to ROCm. An image missing bare sonames
# the gate image has is a difference in US, and it points the wrong way -- the
# canary would look healthier than the gate on the same release, which is the
# ambiguity #382 exists to remove.
#
# See Dockerfile.ci-gpu for the mechanism, the measurements and the two consumers
# (hipcc links against bare names, Proton dlopens them). Nothing in the current
# nightly_eval matrix compiles HIP or enables Proton, so today this changes
# nothing observable here -- it is the image matching the gate rather than a
# repair. That is the point: `rocm/pytorch:latest` resolves to a 7.14 wheel image
# today and will move to ROCm 10, and config/ci/nightly_eval_matrix.yaml is
# explicitly written to grow rows (HRX, which does shell out to hipcc, is parked
# there pending gfx950 recipes). Both changes would otherwise land on a lane that
# had quietly stopped matching.
#
# The layout-aware skip carries more weight here than in the other two files.
# Their bases are tag-pinned and known to be wheel images; this one is whatever
# BASE_IMAGE was resolved to, which may legitimately be a classic /opt/rocm
# install that ships its own devel symlinks. An empty discovery is therefore a
# normal outcome to be skipped, never a build failure -- rocm_layout_guard.py
# above is what fails a base we genuinely cannot read.
#
# Byte-identical to the block in both other Dockerfiles, and
# `test_devel_symlink_fixup_is_identical_across_the_rocm_images` holds all three
# to that. A near-copy is how the dev image drifted into shipping only the
# libamdhip64 fixup while the gate had the full farm.
#
# Deliberately WITHOUT the CI image's hipcc-link and Proton-dlopen smoke tests.
# Those install nothing, so they are not part of "match the gated image" -- they
# only decide whether a build aborts, and this lane's gating semantics are the
# opposite of the gate's. A failed RUN here costs the run its workload verdicts:
# the workflow synthesises an attributable row, but an empty one, for a base
# whose PyTorch workloads might have run perfectly well. Failing closed on
# something no wired canary recipe exercises trades the report this lane exists
# to produce for a capability check the gate already makes at digest-bump time.
# Add them here when the matrix wires a recipe that needs hipcc or Proton.
RUN set -eu; \
    libdirs="$(python -c 'import importlib.util, os; print(chr(32).join(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(s.origin), "lib") for s in (importlib.util.find_spec(m) for m in ("_rocm_sdk_core", "_rocm_sdk_libraries")) if s and s.origin))')"; \
    if [ -z "${libdirs}" ]; then \
      echo "no ROCm wheels (classic layout): skipping devel-symlink fixup"; \
    else \
      created=0; \
      for libdir in ${libdirs}; do \
        [ -d "${libdir}" ] || { echo "resolved ROCm lib dir does not exist: ${libdir}" >&2; exit 1; }; \
        cd "${libdir}"; \
        for versioned in *.so.*; do \
          [ -f "${versioned}" ] || continue; \
          bare="${versioned%%.so.*}.so"; \
          [ -e "${bare}" ] && continue; \
          newest="$(ls -1 "${bare}".* | sort -V | tail -n1)"; \
          ln -s "${newest}" "${bare}"; \
          created=$((created + 1)); \
          echo "created ${libdir}/${bare} -> ${newest}"; \
        done; \
      done; \
      echo "devel-symlink fixup: created ${created} bare sonames"; \
    fi

# Match the gated image's extra deps so a canary/gate difference is attributable
# to ROCm rather than to a missing report dependency. Unpinned here (the gate
# pins them): pinning against a moving base is what would actually break, since
# a new base may not have wheels for a year-old pin.
RUN python -m pip install --no-cache-dir openpyxl seaborn

# No `ENV ROCM_HOME=/opt/rocm` -- see Dockerfile.ci-gpu. It matters more here
# than anywhere else, and for a reason specific to BASE_IMAGE being an argument:
# this file cannot know its base's layout at all. `rocm/pytorch:latest` is a
# wheel image with no /opt/rocm today, and a future one may ship a compat stub or
# be a classic install outright -- ROCM_HOME outranks autodetection in every one
# of those cases, so asserting it is the one thing that could still defeat
# layout-agnostic discovery (#381, #382 design notes).
#
# And no profile script either, unlike Dockerfile.rocm-latest. That script exists
# to put the ROCm lib dirs in front of a HUMAN opening an interactive shell, who
# has no workflow step exporting them. This lane has no interactive shells; it
# also has no reason to differ from the image it exists to predict,
# and Dockerfile.ci-gpu does not ship one. Adding environment here that the gate
# does not have would reintroduce exactly the canary/gate difference the fixup
# above was ported to remove.

WORKDIR /workspace/aorta

CMD ["/bin/bash"]
